          "note": "Transliterations follow Wiktionary's Bengali romanization module (CLAUDE.md §14)."
        }
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "verb-person-honor",
      "number": "44",
      "heading": "Verb Endings Follow Address",
      "statement": "Finite verbs agree for person and honor, not for number. The five endings attach to the pronoun grades of ¶35–¶38: আমি **-ি**, তুই **-িস**, তুমি **-ো**, সে **-ে**, আপনি / তিনি **-েন**.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "আমি করি",
          "roman": "ami kori",
          "gloss": "I do"
        },
        {
          "target": "তুই করিস",
          "roman": "tui koris",
          "gloss": "you (VF) do"
        },
        {
          "target": "তুমি করো",
          "roman": "tumi koro",
          "gloss": "you (familiar) do"
        },
        {
          "target": "সে করে",
          "roman": "se kore",
          "gloss": "he/she (familiar) does"
        },
        {
          "target": "আপনি করেন",
          "roman": "apni koren",
          "gloss": "you (polite) do"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [
        "আমরা করি and তোমরা করো keep the singular ending of their grade; plurality stays in the pronoun (¶36–¶37)."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "verbal-noun",
        "three-address",
        "address-verb-preview",
        "third-person-honor"
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Wikipedia contributors, \"Bengali grammar\", *Wikipedia*, retrieved 13 August 2026.",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengali_grammar",
