        }
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "negation-na",
      "number": "48",
      "heading": "Negation with না",
      "statement": "The particle **না** (na) negates a finite verb and usually follows it: আমি করি না. It is the ordinary negation in the simple present.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "আমি যাই না",
          "roman": "ami jai na",
          "gloss": "I do not go"
        },
        {
          "target": "সে লেখে না",
          "roman": "se lekhe na",
          "gloss": "he/she does not write"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [
        "Perfect tenses use a different negative (-নি); that is outside this section."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "simple-present"
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Wikipedia contributors, \"Bengali grammar\", *Wikipedia*, retrieved 13 August 2026.",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengali_grammar",
          "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sov-order",
      "number": "49",
      "heading": "Subject–Object–Verb",
      "statement": "The finite verb stands at the end of the clause. The subject comes first and objects and locatives precede the verb: আমি বইটা পড়ি.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "আমি বইটা পড়ি",
          "roman": "ami boiṭa poṛi",
          "gloss": "I read the book"
        },
        {
          "target": "সে ঘরে যায়",
          "roman": "se ghore jay",
          "gloss": "he/she goes into the room"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [],
